- BG | ask the elders to give the mask to your own kin's best dancer instead | v: security, benevolence | chance: 70
- BR | turn the mask down, and win the outer fires with a telling of your own | grants: a fringe slot | door | v: achievement, stimulation | chance: 70
works: By the last night of the gathering it has gone the way {N} hoped, and the whole band walks a little taller.
works: The elders of the clans speak {Ns} name with respect, and the band talks of that summer for years.
fails: On the last night the elders of the clans turn to {rival}, and {N} watches from among the drummers.
fails: The summer goes wrong from the first dance, and the band walks home quieter than it came.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 69. the mimic who mocks the chief (pairs D2b)
# A fall. The answers are the band's own: pardon, praise, a following, defiance, stepping back (Green drops the lead),
# leaving, a gift, the old ones' counsel. Chances differ by act and are set so every color's mean is the same.

== the mimic who mocks the chief
tier: life event
only: tribal
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-95
holds: professional actor | amateur actor | lead actor or actress
per_year: 0 0 .02 .02 .02 .01
drivers: unrest+.2
times: per player in the tribal world a year: about 1 in 50 (estimate)
likelier: a chief who has made hard choices, a winter of short tempers, a player with a gift for voices
rarer: a well-loved chief, a band that keeps its tellings to the old stories, a player careful whom the mimic touches
gap: 5-10
tone: trouble
life: community, work
horizon: months
roles: elder, rival, friend
tribal: the whole band laughs at your mimic of the chief, and the chief rises and walks out of the firelight
scene tribal: In the midwinter telling {N} plays the greedy chief of the old story, and gives him the real chief's walk, his cough and his way of eating first. The band roars with laughter. The chief rises without a word and walks out of the firelight, and the laughter dies away.
scene tribal W: The chief is the chief, and a telling is no place to shame him. {N} knows a line was crossed, and that the band will be watching what comes next.
scene tribal U: The old stories have always laughed at greedy chiefs; that is what they are for. {N} wonders how this chief could be brought to see it that way.
scene tribal B: Half the band laughed until they cried, and some of them looked at {N} afterwards in a new way. A chief who cannot take a joke is a chief who can be weakened.
scene tribal R: {N} is still burning from the roar of the laughter. It was the best night at the fire in years, and every word of it was true.
scene tribal G: The band's peace has been broken, and {N} broke it. {N} thinks of the families who must live beside the chief's fire all winter.
- W | go to the chief's fire at dawn, and ask his pardon before the elders | mark: owned up | v: conformity, tradition | chance: 65
- U | at the next telling, turn the mimic into a song in the chief's praise | v: self-direction, achievement | chance: 62
- B | count who laughed loudest, and gather them around your own fire | mark: made an enemy | v: power | chance: 62
- R | play the mimic again at the next fire, louder | mark: defied an authority | closed: approval: mocking the chief a second time; backfire: the chief bars the player from the fire for a season | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 65
- G | step back from the tellings, and keep away from the chief's fire for a season | drops: lead actor or actress | v: security, tradition | chance: 62
- RG | walk with your own kin to the river band, where the chief's word does not reach | mark: left home | moves: yes | v: self-direction, tradition | chance: 65
- UB | find out who carried the tale to the chief, and quietly settle the score | v: power, security | chance: 65
- UG | ask the oldest teller what the bands of old did when a chief was mocked | door | v: universalism, tradition | chance: 65
- WB | send the chief the best hide from your next hunt, with words of respect | v: security, conformity | chance: 65
- WR | tell the chief to his face that the fire has always laughed at chiefs | mark: defied an authority | identity | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 60
works: By the thaw the anger has cooled, and things have settled the way {N} hoped.
works: The band talks about that night for years, and in the end the stories remember {N} kindly.
fails: The chief's anger does not cool, and {N} finds fewer places kept at the fire.
fails: {rival} carries every word to the chief's fire, and the winter grows long and cold for {N}.

# ================================================================ magic world
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 70. the illusionist players come to town (triads D6a)
# A door, open to anyone of age in the magic world (no holds). Each one-color way onto the wagons is a player's place
# (title: professional actor) at the same real chance, for one of the town's own players ([amateur actor]: magic
# carries someone one rung, never from nothing). The triads are the town's other ways in, open to all:
# its own players beside the company, the procession crowd, the children's chaperone, small parts, and watching. The
# fairy scene's children play only with a parent's leave and a chaperone; no option here is a child's.
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (checklist P9): at 5, each player's place on the wagons is a long shot (a bold try at the next
# rung, for the town's own players) and carries `grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed`.

== the illusionist players come to town
tier: life event
only: magic
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-90
per_year: 0 .005 .005 .005 .0025 .0025
drivers: prosper+.3
times: per person in the magic world a year: about 1 in 100 (estimate)
likelier: a prosperous summer, a town on the high road between cities, a fair or a feast day
rarer: hard years, a town off the roads, a strict Order that frowns on wagon players
gap: 5-10
tone: hope
life: work, leisure
horizon: week
roles: friend, parent, elder
magic: painted wagons in the square, and a dragon of light circling the town hall at dusk
scene magic: The illusionist players roll into the square in six painted wagons, and by dusk a dragon of light is circling the town hall while the children shriek below. The company master has nailed a notice to the well: players, hands and walk-ons wanted for the week, and children for the fairy scene, each with a parent's leave and a chaperone.
scene magic W: The company has a licence from the Order, sealed and hung on the first wagon, and a list of rules for everyone who travels with it. {N} reads every line of it twice.
scene magic U: {N} has never seen a glamour up close. How does a dragon hold its shape in the air, and who decides where it flies?
scene magic B: The company master pays by the night, the notice says, and more for anyone who can carry a part. {N} works out what a summer on the road might be worth.
scene magic R: The wagons, the lamps, the dragon: {N} has wanted something like this for years without knowing it. The company leaves at the end of the week.
scene magic G: Half the town is in the square, {friend} among them, and the old folk say the illusionists came once before, long ago, and played the town's own stories.
