- U | learn two of their plays by heart overnight, and show the company master at dawn | title: professional actor | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | requires: amateur actor | without: impossible | mark: learned a skill | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 5
- B | haggle for a player's share of the takings before signing on | title: professional actor | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | requires: amateur actor | without: impossible | v: power, achievement | chance: 5
- R | sign on the spot, and leave with the wagons at the end of the week | title: professional actor | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | requires: amateur actor | without: impossible | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 5
- G | join for the summer, and bring the town's old songs into their plays | title: professional actor | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | requires: amateur actor | without: impossible | aims: professional actor | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 5
- WUR | ask the company to let the town's mystery players play a scene with them | title: amateur actor | door | v: universalism, stimulation | chance: 75
- WBR | walk in the procession under the dragon each night, paid by the night | title: background artist | v: stimulation, security | chance: 75
- WBG | sign on as chaperone for the town's children in the fairy scene | grants: cleared to work with children | v: benevolence, security | chance: 75
- URG | take whatever small parts they need each night, and learn from the players | grants: acting | habit | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 75
- UBG | watch from the crowd every night, and work out how a glamour holds its shape | v: self-direction, security | chance: 75
works: By the time the wagons roll on, the week has given {N} just what {N} hoped for.
works: The company master remembers {Ns} name, and the dragon of light circles the town hall one last time.
fails: The company has all it needs by the second night, and {N} watches the rest of the week from the edge of the crowd.
fails: The week passes in a blur of rain and missed chances, and the wagons roll out at dawn without {N}.

# ------------------------------------------------------- 71. the theatre where the dead come to watch (triads D6b)
# A magic crossing to the lead. The house master chooses the night's lead from three players, so every one-color way is
# the same real chance (30); White's keeps the Order's rules and leaves the token, and still takes a wild risk. The
# triads refuse the night, prepare for it, keep the young players safe, have the company's medium call the dead down
# into the play (round 5, 2026-10-07: someone else's magic, on a closed: approval: line, in place of the person's own
# gift), or go looking for one's own dead.

== the theatre where the dead come to watch
tier: life event
only: magic
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-95
holds: professional actor | lead actor or actress
tenure: 2-100
per_year: 0 0 .005 .005 .005 .005
drivers: fortune+.2
times: per working player (professional or lead) of two years or more in the magic world a year: about 1 in 200 (estimate)
likelier: a company that plays the old playhouses, a player the house master has watched, the turn of the year toward the long nights
rarer: a company that keeps to the new halls, a player new to the city, a year when the Order shuts the old house
gap: 10-20
tone: mixed
life: work, meaning
horizon: week
roles: dead, rival, friend, elder
magic: the old playhouse on the night of the dead, and every seat in the gallery is taken by someone long gone
scene magic: On the night of the dead the old playhouse opens its gallery, and every seat fills with people long gone, pale and patient, in the clothes of their own times. The house master will choose the lead for that one night from three players, and {N} is one of them. Whoever leads it, the city says, is remembered by both worlds.
scene magic W: The Order's rules for the night are old and strict: a token for the dead at the stage door, no names spoken from the stage, the lamps never fully out. {N} knows every one of them.
scene magic U: The house keeps a record of every night of the dead for three hundred years: who played, what the gallery did, who was never the same after. {N} wants to read all of it.
scene magic B: The fee for the night is ten times a season's wage, and the fame of it lasts a lifetime. {rival} wants it as badly as {N} does.
scene magic R: To play to the living and the dead at once, with the whole gallery leaning forward: {N} can hardly breathe for wanting it.
scene magic G: {N} cannot stop thinking about one face that might be in the gallery: {dead}, gone these many years, who never once saw {N} on a stage.
