- WUB | learn the lead's part as its second, and wait in the wings that night | grants: learning lines | habit | binds | v: achievement, conformity | chance: 70
- WRG | take a small part that night, and keep the young players close by the lamps | binds | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 70
- UBR | ask the company's medium to call the dead down from the gallery into the play | grants: good notices | mark: took a wild risk | closed: approval: calling the dead onto the stage without the Order's leave; backfire: nothing answers but a cold wind, and the house master shuts the old playhouse to the company for a year | v: stimulation, power | chance: 70
- BRG | climb to the gallery after the curtain, and look for your own dead | mark: took a wild risk | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 70
works: When the lamps come up the gallery is empty again, and the night has gone the way {N} hoped.
works: Years later the old players still speak of that night of the dead, and of {N}.
fails: The night goes cold and strange, and {N} leaves the playhouse before the lamps are out.
fails: The house master gives the night to {rival}, and {N} hears the gallery's silence from the street.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 72. a glamour that makes the play real (pairs D1a)
# The magic crossing to the lead, with a price or without. Black pays the illusionist's price (the glamour, 35); Red
# wears a glamour bought from a hedge-witch for the night (round 5, 2026-10-07: someone else's magic, on a closed:
# approval: line, in place of the person's own gift; 30); the plain way, on craft and
# rehearsal alone, is the WU pair (12). The other acts report, question, refuse, keep or sell the offer. The pairs form
# a ring (each color in two), so the chances are set around the three parts until every color's mean is the same.
# It comes to a working player, or to an amateur of five years with a lead behind them (LIFE_STAGE): magic carries
# someone one rung, never from nothing.

== a glamour that makes the play real
tier: life event
only: magic
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-95
holds: professional actor | voice actor | understudy | amateur actor
per_year: 0 0 .01 .01 .01 .005
drivers: fortune+.2 prosper+.1
times: per working player (professional or voice) of two years or more, or amateur of five years with a lead behind them, in the magic world a year: about 1 in 100 (estimate)
likelier: a first try-out in a lead, a city where glamours are sold under the counter, a player with more nerves than nights on stage
rarer: a company the Order watches closely, a player long settled in the lead, a house master who searches the dressing rooms
gap: 5-10
tone: mixed
life: work, meaning
horizon: week
roles: rival, mentor, friend
magic: an illusionist in a velvet coat, in your dressing room, with a small silver bell
scene magic: The night before {Ns} first try-out in a lead, an illusionist in a velvet coat is waiting in the dressing room with a small silver bell. One ring before the curtain, he says, and the house will see and feel the play as if it were real: they will weep, and they will not forget. He does not say what it costs, only that it asks something of the player.
scene magic W: Glamours on a public stage need the Order's licence, and this one carries none that {N} can see. There are rules for this, and good reasons for them.
scene magic U: Every glamour draws its power from somewhere. {N} wants to know exactly where this one draws it from before touching the bell.
scene magic B: If the house loves the try-out, the lead is {Ns} for the whole season. {rival} would ring that bell without a second thought, and {N} knows it.
scene magic R: {N} can feel the pull of it already: one ring, and the whole house in the palm of a hand. Or there is the hedge-witch in the lanes behind the playhouse, who sells a night's glamour and asks no questions.
scene magic G: The company has played the plain way for forty years, and {mentor} always says a house can tell when it is being cheated.
- W | report the illusionist to the Order, which licenses every glamour in the city | v: conformity, security | chance: 64
- U | make the illusionist write down exactly what the glamour takes, before any answer | v: self-direction, security | chance: 84
- B | pay the illusionist's price, and play the try-out under the glamour | title: lead actor or actress; professional actor | mark: took a wild risk | binds | v: power, achievement | chance: 35
- R | wear a glamour bought from a hedge-witch for the night, and go on | title: lead actor or actress; professional actor | mark: took a wild risk | closed: approval: an unlicensed glamour on a public stage; backfire: the glamour breaks in the first scene, the whole house sees it, and the company lets the player go | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 30
- G | send the illusionist away, and warn the rest of the company about him | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 50
- BG | keep the illusionist's card, for a night when the company needs it more | door | self_control: + | v: security, power | chance: 80
- BR | sell the story of the illusionist's offer to the broadsheets | mark: made an enemy | v: achievement, stimulation | chance: 80
- UG | ask the company's oldest player what glamours have taken from players before | v: universalism, tradition | chance: 50
- WR | throw the illusionist out of the dressing room, bell and all | v: universalism, stimulation | chance: 90
- WU | play the try-out plain, on craft and long rehearsal alone | title: lead actor or actress; professional actor | self_control: + | v: conformity, achievement | chance: 12
works: The curtain comes down to a silence, then a roar, and the night ends the way {N} wanted.
works: Long after the season ends, {N} is glad of the choice made in that dressing room.
fails: The try-out goes wrong from the first scene, and the house master gives the season's lead to {rival}.
fails: Something of that night stays wrong for a long time, and {N} is never quite sure what it cost.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 73. the mask that changes its wearer (pairs D1b)
# A magic door: the lead in the mask. Wearing it once is the amateur lead (the UR pair); a company master who sees the
# mask at the open trial gives a player's place (the WB pair, 25); Red wears it always, and is changed. The others give
# it to the Order, study it, sell it, break it, drown it, or take it home to its family. The pairs form a ring, so the
# chances are set around the two parts until every color's mean is the same.
# The player's place is one rung up, for a player, an extra or a drama student, never from nothing.

== the mask that changes its wearer
tier: life event
only: magic
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-95
holds: professional actor | amateur actor | background artist | drama school student
tenure: 1-100
per_year: 0 .01 .01 .01 .01 .01
drivers: fortune+.3
times: per player, extra or drama student of a year or more in the magic world a year: about 1 in 100 (estimate)
likelier: an old playhouse or guild hall with trunks nobody has opened in years, a company that buys old costumes, a player restless in small parts
rarer: a new company with new costumes, a player who never goes near the wardrobe, a city where the Order searches every trunk
gap: 5-10
tone: mixed
life: work, meaning
horizon: months
roles: friend, mentor, rival, elder
magic: a mask of white lacquer at the bottom of the costume trunk, warm to the touch
scene magic: At the bottom of an old costume trunk, under moth-eaten cloaks, {N} finds a mask of pale lacquer that is warm to the touch. The wardrobe mistress says it belonged to a famous player, long dead, whose family still lives in the old quarter. Whoever wears it, the story goes, becomes anyone on the stage, and a little more of them each time.
scene magic W: A thing like this belongs with the Order, which keeps such things safe. {N} knows that is what the rules say, whatever the mask might do on a stage.
scene magic U: Who made it, and how? {N} turns the mask over and over, looking for a maker's mark, and finds only a line of tiny letters in no tongue {N} knows.
scene magic B: Collectors of strange things pay fortunes for less. And worn on the right night, before the right company master, it might be worth more than any fortune.
scene magic R: {N} holds it up to the lamp and feels the warmth spread into {Ns} fingers. One night in it, just one, to be anyone at all.
scene magic G: Old things like this have their own ways, and the wardrobe mistress makes the Order's sign whenever she passes the trunk. {N} thinks of the players the mask must have changed, and of the family it came from.
- W | take the mask to the Order's house, and let them keep it safe | v: conformity, security | chance: 85
- U | study how the mask was made, and wear it only in rehearsal | mark: learned a skill | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 67
- B | sell the mask to a collector of strange things, for a very good price | v: power, achievement | chance: 80
- R | wear it in every play from now on, whatever it changes | mark: took a wild risk | identity | self_control: - | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 67
- G | break the mask, and bury the pieces under the old yew behind the playhouse | v: tradition, security | chance: 57
- UR | wear it once, for one great part, and then put it away | grants: a lead role to remember | self_control: + | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 50
- WB | wear it at the guild's open trial, by the guild's rules, with a company master watching | title: professional actor | v: achievement, conformity | chance: 25
- UB | ask a mask-maker what it is worth, and what it has taken from its wearers | door | self_control: + | v: security, power | chance: 80
- RG | throw it in the river at night, and walk home free of it | v: self-direction, tradition | chance: 80
- WG | give it back to the family of the player it once belonged to | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 70
works: Months later {N} is still sure it was the right choice, and the wardrobe trunk is just a trunk again.
works: The whole company notices something new in {N}, and {N} likes what it is.
fails: The mask turns up again where {N} least expects it, as warm as ever.
fails: Something of the mask stays with {N} for a long time, and {friend} says {N} has not been the same since.
